data.content:
## Migration Analysis

Your code has **4 patterns** that need migration:

| Pattern | Location | Replacement |
|---------|----------|-------------|
| Cancellable work loop | `while (_running)` | `CancellationToken` |
| ThreadAbortException as control flow | `catch` block with rollback | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| Thread.ResetAbort | Preventing rethrow | Remove — not needed with cooperative model |
| Thread.Abort in Stop() | Force-killing the thread | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

### Why `Thread.Abort` fails on .NET 8

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` at runtime. The fundamental shift is from **preemptive** (runtime injects an exception) to **cooperative** (code voluntarily checks for cancellation).

### Key pitfalls in YOUR code

1. **`GetNextItem()` may block** — if it blocks indefinitely, cancellation stalls unless you pass the token through or use a cancellable wait internally.
2. **Rollback logic in the catch block** — this cleanup must be preserved; move to `catch (OperationCanceledException)`.
3. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` in `Stop()`** — replace with `Thread.Join(timeout)` or token-based waiting.
4. **`CancellationTokenSource` must be disposed** — wrap in `IDisposable` or dispose in `Stop()`.

### Migrated code for .NET 8

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking calls can be interrupted
                token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint before expensive work
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — migrated from ThreadAbortException catch
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit the loop (replaces Thread.ResetAbort + loop condition)
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation (replaces Thread.Abort)

        // Give the thread time to finish gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose(); // CancellationTokenSource is IDisposable — always dispose
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

### What changed and why

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationTokenSource` + token | Unified cancellation model; token can also interrupt blocking calls |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` | The `when` filter avoids catching unrelated OCEs from other sources |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | `break` | Cooperative model — you control whether the loop continues |
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Signals all token checkpoints and cancellable waits |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | `_workerThread.Join(2s)` | Actually waits for the thread instead of hoping it finishes |
| (none) | `IDisposable` | Required to clean up `CancellationTokenSource` timer/callback leaks |

### Critical: Update `GetNextItem`

If `GetNextItem()` blocks (e.g., on a queue), **you must pass the token through**, otherwise cancellation stalls until it unblocks on its own:

```csharp
// Before — blocks forever, ignores cancellation
private Item GetNextItem()
{
    return _queue.Take(); // no way to interrupt
}

// After — wakes immediately on cancellation
private Item GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    return _queue.Take(token); // BlockingCollection<T>.Take supports CancellationToken
}
```

If you're using `Thread.Sleep` or `WaitHandle.WaitOne` inside `GetNextItem`, replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` so cancellation can interrupt the wait.
